 -- @MAINTAINER@  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 22:42:51 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (4.0.4+git20261018.96.8741a76d-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * The firewall comes on for systems installed before it was turned on at
    install (docs/6, choice 40): on an upgrade, @DISTRO_ID@-config's postinst
    allows LocalSend's port and enables ufw when it is off and the system
    has never had the firewall set up. A user who turns ufw off afterwards
    keeps it off.
  * Docker Desktop is the first entry of Install > Development.
  * Setup > Reset Computer is back on btrfs installs (docs/6, choice 87):
    @DISTRO_ID@-system-factory-reset returns the machine to the installer's
    @factory snapshot, wipes the owner's accounts and data on the next
    boot, and asks for a new owner on tty1 before the login screen,
    re-keying an encrypted disk to the new owner's password. Needs
    @DISTRO_ID@-installer 0.1.8 or later for the snapshot.
  * A keyboard layout chosen in the installer reaches the desktop:
    Hyprland reads /etc/default/keyboard, where Ubuntu keeps it.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 16:51:52 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (4.0.4+git20261017.93.86706132-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * An update no longer leaves the running session with an empty bar and
    dead menu keys. The shell's commands (@DISTRO_ID@-shell,
    @DISTRO_ID@-restart-shell, @DISTRO_ID@-launch-shell) and
    @DISTRO_ID@-update-available fall back to /usr/share/@DISTRO_ID@ when
    OMARCHY_PATH is not set, as in a session that began before the update
    that introduced it, so the update's shell restart works there; the
    update also puts OMARCHY_PATH into such a session's user manager, and
    says the plugins were reloaded only after the restart succeeded.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 15:37:20 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (4.0.4+git20261017.92.3742329d-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * The installer sets the wireless regulatory domain from the timezone
    chosen at install again, as Omarchy does: the timezone's country goes to
    /etc/modprobe.d/@DISTRO_ID@-wireless-regdom.conf as cfg80211's
    ieee80211_regdom option. Wi-Fi cards that manage their own regulatory
    domain, such as Intel's, ignore it.
  * OBS Studio can be installed from the menu: Install > Productivity >
    OBS Studio installs the OBS project's Flatpak from Flathub, and Remove >
    Productivity > OBS Studio removes it with its data.
  * Xournal++ and Kdenlive can be installed from the menu the same way:
    Install > Productivity > Xournal++ and > Kdenlive install their
    projects' Flatpaks from Flathub, and Remove > Productivity removes them.
  * Docker Desktop can be installed from the menu: Install > Development >
    Docker Desktop follows Docker's instructions for Ubuntu (gnome-terminal,
    Docker's apt repository, Docker's package, the kvm group), and Remove >
    Development > Docker Desktop removes it with its containers and images.
    Omarchy's own Docker setup stays out.
  * Plugins and themes written for Omarchy work on the distro (docs/6
    choice 86). Every command answers to its Omarchy name too
    (omarchy-<name> and omarchy link to the distro's), and so does
    /usr/share/omarchy; each user gets ~/.config/omarchy,
    ~/.local/state/omarchy and ~/.local/share/omarchy as links. Names users
    do not see keep Omarchy's spelling: the built-in plugins' IDs
    (omarchy.clock), OMARCHY_* variables, app IDs (org.omarchy.terminal), a
    plugin manifest's "omarchy" section and the icon font's family. A
    migration updates existing users' files to those names, and
    @DISTRO_ID@-config needs @DISTRO_ID@-session 0.1.7, which exports the
    GPU variables under Omarchy's names.
  * Adding a plugin or a theme warns when its files mention Arch Linux's
    package tools (pacman, yay, paru, makepkg), and asks before keeping
    it. Stand-ins for those tools, last on PATH, say that the distro uses
    apt and change nothing. Omarchy's package commands (add, present,
    missing) use Ubuntu's packages for 162 Arch names, listed in
    /usr/share/@DISTRO_ID@/default/@DISTRO_ID@/arch-package-names and drawn
    from a scan of every plugin in Omarchy's marketplace (bluez-obex is
    bluez-obexd).

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:01:20 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (4.0.4+git20261017.78.dacbe9f9-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Style > Font changes the system's monospace font again: the fontconfig
    rule that makes monospace JetBrains Mono is linked as
    /etc/fonts/conf.d/51-@DISTRO_ID@.conf, after 50-user.conf, so the user's
    choice in ~/.config/fontconfig/fonts.conf comes first. Before, the rule
    ran first and the choice never applied; the menu's checkmark and
    fc-match monospace always named JetBrains Mono.
  * Style > Font no longer lists icon fonts such as Material Icons, which
    drew icons in place of letters when chosen.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 23:45:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (4.0.4+git20261017.77.8ae5b19b-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * @DISTRO_ID@-update offers a reboot when a package created
    /run/reboot-required, Ubuntu's way for a package to ask for one.
  * @DISTRO_ID@-config's postinst asks for a reboot that way when it removes
    the Wi-Fi 7 fix from a machine it does not belong to.
  * More hardware fixes for other machines, at install on a device match:
    initramfs hooks that add the one keyboard module Ubuntu's initramfs
    lacks for the disk encryption passphrase prompt (Surface:
    surface_aggregator_hub; MacBook8,1: spi_pxa2xx_pci), libcamera for the
    Intel IPU7 camera (OV08X40), the Dell XPS haptic touchpad package with
    Hardware > Touchpad Haptics, qmk_hid on the Framework Laptop 16, and
    asusctl on ASUS ROG laptops.
  * Hardware > Hybrid GPU is back on ASUS ROG laptops with two GPUs: it
    installs supergfxctl and switches between Hybrid and Integrated. Its
    scripts call GNU's gnutimeout, because Ubuntu's timeout does not stop a
    wedged supergfxctl's child processes.
  * mise is preinstalled from mise's own apt repository (mise.jdx.dev/deb),
    as Omarchy preinstalls it, and apt updates it. The port's own
    Install > Development > mise entry and oaxaca-mise-ensure are gone; the
    commands that use mise call it as Omarchy's do.
  * dua-cli is preinstalled again with Omarchy's Disk Usage launcher, which
    opens "dua i /" in a floating terminal; a migration adds the launcher
    for existing users.
  * oaxaca-launch-openclaw, oaxaca-openclaw-onboard and oaxaca-install-font
    are gone: OpenClaw and the extra Nerd Font installers are not part of
    the desktop, and nothing called these commands.
  * Install and Remove > Gaming > Heroic (Epic Games) are back, from
    Flathub.
  * Install and Remove > Gaming > Minecraft are back: Mojang's own .deb,
    installed with @DISTRO_ID@-gdk-pixbuf-compat, which provides the
    GdkPixbuf library's old package name that Mojang's package needs.
  * Install > Service > NordVPN and > Bitwarden are back, from the
    vendors' own snaps.
  * TCP MTU probing is on again (99-@DISTRO_ID@-sysctl.conf,
    net.ipv4.tcp_mtu_probing=1), so connections recover on networks that
    drop path-MTU messages.
  * Install > Development > Herdr and Install > Service > cliamp install
    Omarchy's herdr and cliamp through mise, with Omarchy's bindings
    (Super+Ctrl+Return, Super+Ctrl+K, Super+Shift+Alt+M), the h alias and
    Learn > Herdr.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:02:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (4.0.4+git20261016.58.e69c287c-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Share > Receive opens LocalSend to receive files, and Install >
    Service > Chromium Account lets Chromium sign in to a Google account.
  * The ic, ix and icx aliases open tdl's tmux layout with OpenCode,
    Claude Code or both.
  * After the first update, a one-time notification offers to install
    dictation with Voxtype.
  * Install > Service > 1Password also installs its Chromium extension;
    the removal takes the extension out.
  * Fingerprint authentication skips the reader while the laptop lid is
    closed: Setup > Security > Fingerprint enables the new pam-auth-update
    profile @DISTRO_ID@-fprintd, Ubuntu's fprintd profile with a pam_exec
    gate in front. Run the setup again to switch an existing setup over.
    The package's prerm removes both of its PAM profiles.
  * Setup > DNS also sets NetworkManager's global DNS, so networks joined
    later use the chosen servers. A polkit rule
    (50-@DISTRO_ID@-global-dns.rules) lets the users who may change network
    connections change it without a password, as for the connections.
  * Hardware fixes for other machines, applied at install on a device
    match: the xe options for ASUS ExpertBook B9406 and Zenbook UX5406AA
    displays, Intel Quick Sync and i965 video drivers, and b43 firmware
    for the Broadcom BCM4331.
  * The inotify watch limit is 524288 again (90-@DISTRO_ID@-file-watchers.conf),
    over localsearch's 65536.
  * whois is preinstalled.
  * The Wi-Fi 7 fix (iwlwifi disable_11be) applies to Dell XPS laptops on
    Panther Lake only; the postinst removes it from other machines with an
    Intel BE200 or BE211, which get Wi-Fi 7 back at the next boot.
  * The maintainer scripts call programs by name (Debian Policy 6.1), and
    lintian overrides with reasons cover the sample hooks, the copied
    first-run hooks, the shell's sanitizer strings, the migration runner's
    dpkg lock wait and the commands without manual pages.
  * The pacman sample hook (pre-refresh-pacman.d) is gone: Ubuntu has no
    pacman and the port has no "refresh pacman".

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:01:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (4.0.4+git20261016.53.d0a9b09a-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * The firewall is on: apply-system allows LocalSend's port (53317 tcp
    and udp) and turns ufw on for the next boot, on Ubuntu's policies
    (deny incoming), without Omarchy's Docker rules. Systems installed
    earlier keep ufw off.
  * Install and Remove > Development no longer offer PHP or Laravel.
  * Wi-Fi power save is off again (wifi.powersave = 2 in
    /etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/@DISTRO_ID@-wifi-powersave.conf): power save
    causes latency spikes and link drops on Intel BE200/BE211 firmware.
  * btop, starship, eza, zoxide, lazygit and inxi are preinstalled again,
    with Omarchy's btop, lazygit and starship configurations, btop's theme
    sync and the Activity binding (Super+Ctrl+T); a migration seeds the
    configurations for existing users. Depend on ttfx, so the screensaver
    runs. The clipboard's image action and imv's Ctrl+E open swappy.
  * Pause Hyprland's automatic config reload while @DISTRO_ID@-config is
    unpacked (new preinst) and resume it with one reload in the postinst.
  * systemd-oomd may kill the user's apps (app.slice) but no longer the
    compositor: Ubuntu's kill policy on user@.service is set back to auto.
  * SSH keepalive for every host (ServerAliveInterval 15), so the shell's
    ssh wrapper notices a dropped connection.
  * unzip, GNOME Disks and GNOME Calculator are preinstalled; the
    calculator takes Super+Ctrl+Q and the Calculator key. Drop
    @DISTRO_ID@-desktop-apps, whose only application moved here.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (@OMARCHY_VERSION@+git20261016.45.f82dbf50-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Setup > Security > TPM Unlock: opt-in unlock of the encrypted root disk
    by the TPM at boot through Clevis, bound to PCR 7 (Secure Boot state);
    Remove > Security > TPM Unlock takes it off. Depend on clevis-luks,
    clevis-tpm2 and clevis-initramfs.
  * tmux gives extended keys to every xterm-type terminal, so Shift+Enter
    reaches programs such as Claude Code inside tmux in Foot, Alacritty and
    Ghostty; a migration fixes the line in existing users' tmux.conf.
  * @DISTRO_ID@-update takes a Timeshift snapshot of / before updating on
    btrfs installs and keeps the newest five of its own (new
    @DISTRO_ID@-snapshot; restore opens Timeshift).
  * The launcher starts applications whose desktop id ends in .desktop,
    such as Telegram's org.telegram.desktop.
  * The fork's tests are sorted for the port (the fork's PORT.md, Tests).
  * Omarchy's agent skills are shipped again, ported to Ubuntu, and linked
    for Claude Code, Codex, Pi and Hermes (a migration links them for
    existing users); their bug reports go to the fork, santisbon/omarchy,
    whose name the rename step now keeps (DISTRO_FORK_REPO).

